          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            There are many ways to describe a
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          %ul
            %li
              it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              in which all objects have “duals”, but in a weaker sense than in a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            %li
              it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
              in which the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal-hom"} internal-hom
              can be expressed in terms of the tensor product in a particular way.
            %li
              it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linearly+distributive+category"} linearly distributive category
              in which all objects have “duals” in an appropriate linearly distributive sense.
            %li
              it is a semantics for classical multiplicative
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %li
              it is a representable
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-polycategory"} star-polycategory
            %li
              it is a sort of categorified
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius algebra
          %p
            In particular, it has two monoidal structures
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⅋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan%27s+laws"} de Morgan
            duality”:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⅋
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\parr B \cong (A^\ast \otimes B^\ast)^\ast
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+pseudomonoid"} star-autonomous pseudomonoid
            (a.k.a. Frobenius pseudomonoid, since it categorifies a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+bicategory"} monoidal bicategory
          %p
            (Regarding the use of “autonomous”, this was once used as a bare adjective to describe a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+monoidal+category"} compact closed monoidal category
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p There are two useful equivalent formulation of the definition
          #DefByDualizingObject.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                \-autonomous category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\langle
                    C,\otimes, I,\multimap\rangle
              with a
              = succeed ":" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object+in+a+closed+category"} global dualizing object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
              such that the canonical morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_A \;\colon\; A \to (A \multimap \bot) \multimap \bot ,
            %p
              which is the transpose of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation+map"} evaluation map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ev
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⊥
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ev_{A,\bot} \;\colon\; (A \multimap \bot) \otimes A \to \bot\,,
            %p
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊸
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \multimap
              denotes the
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
          #DefByDualization.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                \-autonomous category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi op
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-)^\ast \colon \mathcal{C}^{op} \longrightarrow \mathcal{C}
            %p
              such that there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi C
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{C}(A \otimes B, C^\ast) \simeq \mathcal{C}(A, (B\otimes C)^\ast)
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              These two definitions, def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DefByDualizingObject"}
              and def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DefByDualization"}
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Given def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DefByDualizingObject"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              into the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object+in+a+closed+category"} dualizing object
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-)^ \ast  \coloneqq (-)\multimap\bot
                    \\,.
            %p
              Then the morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_A
              is natural in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d:1\Rightarrow(-)^{**}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi B
                            %mo ,
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi B
                            %mo ,
                            %mi C
                            %mo ⊸
                            %mo ⊥
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                            %mi hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi C
                            %mo ,
                            %mo ⊥
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                            %mi hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ,
                            %mo ⊥
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                            %mi hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⊸
                            %mo ⊥
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mi hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi C
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\hom(A\otimes B,C^*)&amp; = \hom(A\otimes B, C\multimap\bot) \\
                    \&amp; \cong \hom((A\otimes B)\otimes C,\bot) \\
                    \&amp; \cong \hom(A\otimes(B\otimes C), \bot) \\
                    \&amp; \cong \hom(A,(B\otimes C)\multimap\bot) \\
                    \&amp; = \hom(A,(B\otimes C)^*)
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              This yields the structure of def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DefByDualization"}
            %p
              Conversely, given the latter then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizing+object"} dualizing object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
              is defined as the dual of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot \coloneqq I^*
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⅋
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \multimap B \coloneqq A^* \parr B
              (where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⅋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
              is defined as below).
          %p
            (See
            %a{:href => "https://github.com/punkdit/categories/blob/master/www.mta.ca/cat-dist/archive/2007/07-12#L2488-L2574"} this discussion
            on the
            %a{:href => "https://www.mta.ca/~cat-dist/"} categories mailing list
            for alternative definitions and remarks about coherence.)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              A
              = succeed "-autonomous" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              is compatible with duality in that there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (A \otimes B)^\ast \simeq A^\ast \otimes B^\ast
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
          %p
            More generally, even if the
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+duality"} de Morgan duality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi B
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \parr B \coloneqq  (A^\ast \otimes B^\ast)^\ast
                  \\,.
          %p
            making it into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linearly+distributive+category"} linearly distributive category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InternalLogic"} Properties – Internal logic
          %p
            A general
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit"} counit
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
            refer to two different tensor products: we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⅋
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \to A \parr A^*
            but
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^* \otimes A \to \bot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\otimes,\top)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\parr,\bot)
            are two different monoidal structures. The necessary relationship between two such monoidal structures such that this makes sense, i.e. such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangle+identities"} triangle identities
            can be stated, is encoded by a linearly distributive category; then an
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
          %p =–
          %h2#functors_and_transformations Functors and transformations
          %p
            It may not be clear from the above definitions what the appropriate notion of “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            \-autonomous functor” is. In fact, from at least one perspective it suffices to consider ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functors"} lax monoidal functors
            = succeed "-autonomy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mi Aut
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast Aut
            denote the full sub-2-category of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SymMonCat
                    %mi lax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymMonCat_{lax}
            on the (symmetric)
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SymLinDist
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymLinDist
            denote the 2-category of symmetric
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linearly+distributive+categories"} linearly distributive categories
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linearly+distributive+category"} linearly distributive category
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              There is a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mi Aut
                    %mo →
                    %mi SymLinDist
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast Aut \to SymLinDist
              that is 2-fully-faithful, i.e. an equivalence on hom-categories, and has both a left and a right
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-adjoint"} 2-adjoint
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              A linearly distributive functor consists of a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\otimes
              that is lax monoidal for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
              and a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⅋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\parr
              that is lax monoidal for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⅋
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⅋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
              is defined in a
              = succeed "-autonomous" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⅋
                      %mi B
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo *
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mo *
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\parr B = (A^* \otimes B^*)^*
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a lax monoidal functor between
              = succeed "-autonomous" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊗
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\otimes = F
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mo ⅋
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\parr(A) = (F(A^*))^*
              %a{:href => "#CockettSeely99"} Cockett-Seely 1999
              for details.
          %p
            In the non-symmetric case, we need to additionally require of an “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            \-autonomous functor” that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*(F(A^*)) \cong (F({}^*A))^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⅋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\parr
            to be their common value.
          %p
            On the other hand, if we consider instead “Frobenius linear functors” between linearly distributive categories, then such functors necessarily preserve duals. Thus, the corresponding notion of
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(A^*) \cong (F(A))^*
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#InternalLogic Internal logic
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of star-autonomous categories is the multiplicative fragment of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+linear+logic"} classical linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+linear+logic"} classical linear logic
            (
            %a{:href => "#Seely89"}> Seely 89, prop. 1.5
            ). See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A simple example of a
                = succeed "-autonomous" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional vector space
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                itself plays the role of the dualizing object, so that for an f.d. vector space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi V
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^*
                is the usual
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual space
                of linear maps into
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %p
                More generally, any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
                is
                = succeed "-autonomous" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                as the dualizing object.
            %li
              %p
                A more interesting example of a
                = succeed "-autonomous" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sup-lattice"} sup-lattice
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(A, B)
                is itself a sup-lattice, so this category is closed. The free sup-lattice on a poset
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is the internal hom of posets
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msup
